\title{Finite-Key Bounds on a Certified Asymptotic Core\\[0.4em]
\large Rational certificates for the BB84 key rate and a two-sided
inverse-square law for the break-even block size}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{23 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
The Shor--Preskill asymptotic key rate for the BB84 quantum key distribution
protocol, $r(Q) = 1 - 2h(Q)$, is positive below the threshold
$\Qstar = 0.1100278644\ldots$ determined by $h(\Qstar) = 1/2$.  Deployment
decisions are routinely made by comparing a measured quantum bit error rate
against this threshold.  We show that this criterion is quantitatively
misleading, and we quantify the discrepancy exactly.

We develop a certificate calculus that replaces every real-number computation in
the key-rate chain by an exact integer comparison.  At a rational error rate
$Q = a/(a+c)$ the rate satisfies the identity
$(a+c)\,r_\nats(Q) = \log(N/D)$ with $N = 2^{a+c}a^{2a}c^{2c}$ and
$D = (a+c)^{2(a+c)}$ integers.  Combined with the Pad\'e $[1/1]$ lower bound
$\log x \ge 2(x-1)/(x+1)$, a single integer inequality yields an explicit
\emph{rational} lower bound on the rate; a dyadic variant and a hybrid of the two
complete the calculus.  At $Q = 11\%$ an $823$-digit integer comparison certifies
$r \ge 1/6000$ bits per sifted bit.

Feeding these rational cores into the standard finite-key accounting
$L(n) = n\rho - C\sqrt{n\ln(1/\varepsilon)}$ and a repaired leftover-hash bound
yields an end-to-end guarantee: at $Q = 11\%$, with $C = 10$ and
$\varepsilon = 2^{-50}$, the extractable $\varepsilon$-secure key length is
non-positive for every block size $n \le 10^{11}$, while for $n \ge 10^{12}$ it
is at least $n/12000 - 101$ bits.

Our main structural result explains this as a law.  Writing $n^\ast(Q)$ for the
break-even block size, we prove
\[
\frac{C^2\ln(1/\varepsilon)}{44\,(\Qstar - Q)^2} \;\le\; n^\ast(Q) \;\le\;
\frac{C^2\ln(1/\varepsilon)}{9\,(\Qstar - Q)^2}
\]
for every $Q \in [10\%, \Qstar)$, so $n^\ast(Q) = \Theta\big((\Qstar-Q)^{-2}\big)$
with a certified constant ratio $44/9 < 5$.  The lower bound holds for
\emph{every} valid rational rate certificate; the upper bound is witnessed by an
explicit rational certificate.  Both halves follow from one-sided Lipschitz
bounds for the binary entropy proved by the same monotonicity template.  We also
record a vacuity audit of a commonly stated form of the leftover-hash lemma, and
give a repaired version with the same conclusion and satisfiable hypotheses.  A
fully certified six-row parameter table concludes the development.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} BB84, finite-key analysis, Shor--Preskill rate,
binary entropy, Pad\'e approximant, leftover hashing, privacy amplification,
rational certificates.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The threshold and its role}

In the BB84 protocol Alice prepares single photons in one of four polarization
states drawn from two mutually unbiased bases, Bob measures in a randomly chosen
basis, and after public basis reconciliation the two parties retain a
\emph{sifted} string of $n$ bits.  Comparing a random sample reveals the
\emph{quantum bit error rate} (QBER)
\[
Q = \Pr[\text{Alice's bit} \ne \text{Bob's bit}].
\]
Under the standard conservative assumption that all observed noise is
adversarial, the Shor--Preskill analysis gives the asymptotic secret-key rate
\[
r(Q) = 1 - 2h(Q), \qquad h(Q) = -Q\log_2 Q - (1-Q)\log_2(1-Q),
\]
in bits of final key per sifted bit.  In nats,
\[
r_\nats(Q) = \log 2 - 2H(Q), \qquad H(Q) = -Q\log Q - (1-Q)\log(1-Q),
\]
with $r = r_\nats/\log 2$.  The rate is positive precisely on $[0,\Qstar)$, where
$\Qstar$ is the unique solution in $(0,1/2)$ of $h(\Qstar) = 1/2$:
\[
\Qstar = 0.1100278644\ldots
\]
This is the celebrated ``eleven percent'' threshold, used in practice as an
acceptance criterion for a physical link.

\subsection{Two problems with the threshold as a figure of merit}

\paragraph{The certificate problem.}
Near $\Qstar$ the rate is a difference of nearly equal quantities.  At
$Q = 11\%$ one has $h(Q) = 0.49991596\ldots$ and $r(Q) = 1.680837\times10^{-4}$:
the answer is determined by the fourth decimal of a difference of numbers near
$1/2$.  A floating-point evaluation of $r(0.11)$ is not a proof of positivity; it
is a computation whose reliability rests on the rounding behaviour of the
logarithm implementation.

\paragraph{The finiteness problem.}
The rate $r(Q)$ is asymptotic.  Any finite protocol pays a statistical
correction of order $\sqrt{n}$ for the fluctuation of empirical entropy
estimates, plus a constant charge for privacy amplification.  Whether a positive
asymptotic rate translates into a positive \emph{finite} key length at a
realistic $n$ is a separate question --- and near the threshold the answer is
routinely no.

\subsection{Contributions}

\begin{enumerate}
\item A \emph{rational certificate calculus} (\S\ref{sec:cert}) for the
asymptotic rate at rational QBER, in three forms --- dyadic, Pad\'e, hybrid ---
each reducing a rate lower bound to a single exact integer comparison, with no
floating-point arithmetic anywhere.
\item A \emph{vacuity audit and repair} of the leftover-hash bound
(\S\ref{sec:pa}): a widely used hypothesis form is unsatisfiable exactly in the
regime where it is advertised as strong; a repaired hypothesis matching what
two-universal hashing delivers is satisfiable and yields the same conclusion.
\item An \emph{end-to-end finite-key extraction theorem} (\S\ref{sec:fk}) and its
instantiation at $Q = 11\%$: no positive guarantee below $n = 10^{11}$, at least
$n/12000 - 101$ bits above $n = 10^{12}$.
\item A \emph{two-sided inverse-square law} for the break-even block size
(\S\ref{sec:law}), with certified constants $44$ and $9$.
\item A \emph{certified six-row parameter table} (\S\ref{sec:table}) spanning
$Q = 1\%$ to $11\%$, in which the required block size varies by eight orders of
magnitude while the asymptotic rate varies by three and a half.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Preliminaries}

Throughout, $\log$ is the natural logarithm, $\log_2$ the base-two logarithm, and
\[
H(p) = -p\log p - (1-p)\log(1-p), \qquad h(p) = H(p)/\log 2,
\]
with $H(0) = H(1) = 0$.  The function $H$ is continuous on $[0,1]$ and
differentiable on $(0,1)$ with $H'(p) = \log\frac{1-p}{p}$, which is strictly
decreasing, positive on $(0,1/2)$ and zero at $p = 1/2$.

\begin{definition}[Asymptotic key rate]
$r_\nats(Q) = \log 2 - 2H(Q)$ and $r(Q) = r_\nats(Q)/\log 2 = 1 - 2h(Q)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Threshold]
A \emph{threshold} is any $\Qstar \in [0,1/2]$ with $r_\nats(\Qstar) = 0$.  Since
$H$ is strictly increasing on $[0,1/2]$, such a $\Qstar$ is unique, and satisfies
the certified enclosure $0.1100 < \Qstar < 0.1101$.
\end{definition}

All statements below that mention $\Qstar$ assume only that it is a zero of the
rate in the appropriate range; no closed form is used.

\begin{definition}[Finite-key length]
For a rational rate certificate $\rho \in \mathbb{Q}_{>0}$ (bits per sifted bit),
a rational constant $C \in \mathbb{Q}_{\ge 0}$, a block size $n \in \mathbb{N}$
and a security parameter $\varepsilon \in (0,1)$, set
\[
L(\rho,C,n,\varepsilon) = n\rho - C\sqrt{n\ln(1/\varepsilon)},
\qquad
\Lext(\rho,C,n,\varepsilon) = L(\rho,C,n,\varepsilon) - 2\log_2(1/\varepsilon).
\]
\end{definition}

The parameters $\rho$ and $C$ are deliberately rational.  The constant $C$
aggregates the constants of the entropy-fluctuation (asymptotic equipartition)
estimate and enters only through the hypothesis, always stated explicitly and
denoted $\AEP$, that the adversary's min-entropy about the reconciled string is
at least $L(\rho,C,n,\varepsilon)$ bits.

\section{A rational certificate calculus}
\label{sec:cert}

\subsection{The integer identity at rational QBER}

\begin{lemma}[Rational-QBER identity]\label{lem:identity}
Let $a,c$ be positive integers and $Q = a/(a+c)$.  Then
\[
(a+c)\,r_\nats(Q) = \log\frac{N}{D}, \qquad
N = 2^{a+c}a^{2a}c^{2c}, \quad D = (a+c)^{2(a+c)}.
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Write $m = a+c$, so $Q = a/m$ and $1-Q = c/m$.  Then
\[
m\,r_\nats(Q) = m\log 2 + 2a\log\frac{a}{m} + 2c\log\frac{c}{m},
\]
whose exponential is $2^m a^{2a}c^{2c}/m^{2m} = N/D$.
\end{proof}

Both $N$ and $D$ are integers; at $a+c = 100$ they have roughly $400$ digits.
The sign of the rate is the sign of $N - D$, an exact decision.  To obtain a
quantitative bound we need rational lower bounds for $\log$.

\subsection{The Pad\'e $[1/1]$ logarithm bound}

\begin{lemma}[Pad\'e lower bound]\label{lem:pade}
For all real $x \ge 1$, $\displaystyle \log x \ge \frac{2(x-1)}{x+1}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Let $f(x) = \log x - 2(x-1)/(x+1)$.  Then $f(1) = 0$ and
\[
f'(x) = \frac{1}{x} - \frac{4}{(x+1)^2}
= \frac{(x+1)^2 - 4x}{x(x+1)^2} = \frac{(x-1)^2}{x(x+1)^2} \ge 0,
\]
so $f$ is nondecreasing on $[1,\infty)$ and $f(x) \ge f(1) = 0$.
\end{proof}

This is the diagonal Pad\'e approximant of $\log$ at $x=1$; its error is
$O((x-1)^3)$, against $O((x-1)^2)$ for the tangent-line bound
$\log x \ge 1 - 1/x$.

\begin{lemma}[Monotonicity of the Pad\'e functional]\label{lem:mono}
The map $x \mapsto 2(x-1)/(x+1) = 2 - 4/(x+1)$ is nondecreasing on
$[1,\infty)$.
\end{lemma}

\subsection{The three certificate schemes}

\begin{theorem}[Pad\'e certificate]\label{thm:padecert}
Let $a,c,\num,\den$ be natural numbers with $a,c,\den > 0$ and suppose
\[
(\den + \num)\,(a+c)^{2(a+c)} \;\le\; \den\cdot 2^{a+c}a^{2a}c^{2c}.
\]
Then
\[
r_\nats\!\left(\frac{a}{a+c}\right) \;\ge\;
\frac{2\,\num}{(a+c)(2\,\den + \num)}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The hypothesis is $(\den+\num)D \le \den N$, i.e.\ $N/D \ge (\den+\num)/\den \ge 1$.
By Lemma~\ref{lem:mono} followed by Lemma~\ref{lem:pade},
\[
\log\frac{N}{D} \;\ge\;
\frac{2\big(\tfrac{\den+\num}{\den} - 1\big)}{\tfrac{\den+\num}{\den} + 1}
= \frac{2\,\num}{2\,\den + \num},
\]
and Lemma~\ref{lem:identity} divides by $a+c$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Dyadic certificate]\label{thm:dyadic}
If $2^m (a+c)^{2(a+c)} \le 2^{a+c}a^{2a}c^{2c}$ then
$r\big(a/(a+c)\big) \ge m/(a+c)$ bits per sifted bit.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The hypothesis says $N/D \ge 2^m$, so $\log_2(N/D) \ge m$; apply
Lemma~\ref{lem:identity} in base two.
\end{proof}

The dyadic conclusion carries no logarithm constants at all --- $m$ is literally
a certified lower bound for $\log_2(N/D)$ --- but it resolves the rate only to
the nearest $1/(a+c)$ bits and degenerates near threshold, where the optimal $m$
is $0$.  The two schemes are the extreme faces of one family.

\begin{theorem}[Hybrid dyadic--Pad\'e certificate]\label{thm:hybrid}
If
\[
(\den + \num)\cdot 2^m (a+c)^{2(a+c)} \;\le\; \den\cdot 2^{a+c}a^{2a}c^{2c},
\]
then
\[
r_\nats\!\left(\frac{a}{a+c}\right) \;\ge\;
\frac{1}{a+c}\left(m\log 2 + \frac{2\,\num}{2\,\den + \num}\right).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Write $y = N/(2^m D)$; the hypothesis says $y \ge (\den+\num)/\den \ge 1$.  Then
$\log(N/D) = m\log 2 + \log y$, and Lemmas~\ref{lem:pade}--\ref{lem:mono} bound
$\log y$ as in Theorem~\ref{thm:padecert}.
\end{proof}

Setting $\num = 0$ recovers Theorem~\ref{thm:dyadic}; setting $m = 0$ recovers
Theorem~\ref{thm:padecert}.  Both degenerate cases are legal, so the hybrid is a
genuine generalization.

\subsection{The certified rows}

\begin{theorem}[Rate at $Q = 11\%$]\label{thm:eleven}
$r_\nats(11/100) \ge \tfrac{117}{1005850} \approx 1.163199\times 10^{-4}$ nats,
and consequently
\[
r(11/100) \;\ge\; \frac{1}{6000} \quad\text{bits per sifted bit.}
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:padecert} with $a=11$, $c=89$, $\den = 10^4$,
$\num = 117$.  The required inequality $10117\,D \le 10^4 N$ is an exact
comparison between $823$-digit integers, and the conclusion is
$2\cdot117/(100\cdot20117) = 117/1005850$.  For the bit form divide by $\log 2$
using the certified bound $\log 2 < 0.6931471808$.
\end{proof}

The Pad\'e bound is essential rather than cosmetic.  With
$N/D = 1.0117188056\ldots$:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
bound on $\log x$ & $r_\nats(0.11)$ & in bits \\
\midrule
tangent line $1-1/x$ & $1.158307\times10^{-4}$ & $1.671083\times10^{-4}$ \\
Pad\'e $2(x-1)/(x+1)$ & $1.165054\times10^{-4}$ & $1.680818\times10^{-4}$ \\
truth & $1.165067\times10^{-4}$ & $1.680837\times10^{-4}$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The tangent-line bound loses $6.8\times10^{-7}$ nats; the Pad\'e bound loses
$1.3\times10^{-9}$, removing better than $99\%$ of the deficit.  The target
rational $1/6000 = 1.666667\times10^{-4}$ bits is cleared by $0.85\%$ under
Pad\'e but only $0.27\%$ under the tangent line --- too tight to be robust to the
certified rounding of $\log 2$.

\begin{theorem}[Dyadic rows]\label{thm:rows}
With $a+c = 100$ the dyadic certificates hold with $m = 83,71,42,19,6$ at
$Q = 1\%,2\%,5\%,8\%,10\%$, giving
\[
r(0.01)\ge0.83,\quad r(0.02)\ge0.71,\quad r(0.05)\ge0.42,\quad
r(0.08)\ge0.19,\quad r(0.10)\ge0.06 .
\]
Each is sharp for the scheme: $m+1$ fails in every case, since $100\,r$ equals
$83.841$, $71.712$, $42.721$, $19.564$, $6.201$ respectively.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Hybrid row at $Q = 10\%$]\label{thm:ten}
$r(1/10) \ge 62/1000 = 0.0620$ bits per sifted bit.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:hybrid} with $a = 10$, $c = 90$, $m = 6$, $\den = 10^4$,
$\num = 1493$; here $y = N/(2^6D) = 1.1494002612\ldots$ and $y \ge 11493/10000$
is an exact integer comparison.
\end{proof}

The raw hybrid bound is $0.06200432$ against a true value $0.06200881$, reducing
the certificate error from the dyadic $2.0\times10^{-3}$ to $4.5\times10^{-6}$;
rounding down to $62/1000$ still leaves an error of only $8.8\times10^{-6}$.  The
hybrid residual is $O((y-1)^3)$, which explains its uniform sharpness once a
dyadic step brings $y$ close to $1$.

\section{Privacy amplification: a vacuity audit and a repair}
\label{sec:pa}

Let $p : \{0,1\}^\ell \to \mathbb{R}$ be probability-normalized,
$\sum_i p_i = 1$.  The Cauchy--Schwarz core of the leftover-hash lemma is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:cs}
\sum_i \left| p_i - 2^{-\ell}\right| \;\le\; \sqrt{2^\ell \sum_i p_i^2 - 1}.
\end{equation}

\begin{proposition}[Collision probability is at least uniform]\label{prop:coll}
Every normalized $p$ on $\{0,1\}^\ell$ satisfies $\sum_i p_i^2 \ge 2^{-\ell}$.
Nonnegativity of $p$ is not required.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
By Cauchy--Schwarz, $1 = \big(\sum_i 1\cdot p_i\big)^2 \le 2^\ell \sum_i p_i^2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Vacuity]\label{cor:vacuous}
If $\ell < k$, no normalized $p$ on $\{0,1\}^\ell$ satisfies
$\sum_i p_i^2 \le 2^{-k}$.
\end{corollary}

Thus a privacy-amplification statement of the shape ``if $\sum p^2 \le 2^{-k}$
and $\ell < k$ then the output is secure'' is true but has \emph{no instances}:
it establishes nothing about any protocol.  Two-universal hashing does not
deliver $\sum p^2 \le 2^{-k}$; it delivers $\sum p^2 \le 2^{-\ell} + 2^{-k}$.

\begin{theorem}[$\varepsilon$-security of privacy amplification]\label{thm:lhl}
Let $p$ be normalized on $\{0,1\}^\ell$ with
$\sum_i p_i^2 \le 2^{-\ell} + 2^{-k}$, let $\varepsilon > 0$, and suppose the
leftover-hash budget $\ell + 2\log_2(1/\varepsilon) \le k$ holds.  Then
$\sum_i |p_i - 2^{-\ell}| \le \varepsilon$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The algebraic collapse $2^\ell(2^{-\ell} + 2^{-k}) - 1 = 2^{\ell-k}$ is exact.
Substituting into \eqref{eq:cs} gives
$\sum_i|p_i - 2^{-\ell}| \le 2^{(\ell-k)/2}$.  The budget rearranges to
$\ell - k \le 2\log_2\varepsilon$, whence $2^{(\ell-k)/2} \le \varepsilon$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Satisfiability]\label{prop:sat}
For all $\ell,k$ the uniform distribution on $\{0,1\}^\ell$ satisfies both
hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:lhl}: it is normalized and has
$\sum_i p_i^2 = 2^{-\ell} \le 2^{-\ell} + 2^{-k}$.
\end{proposition}

The repaired statement therefore has instances for every parameter choice, gives
the same conclusion, and derives the budget from the algebra rather than
imposing it.  At $\varepsilon = 2^{-50}$ the charge is exactly $100$ bits.

\section{Finite-key extraction}
\label{sec:fk}

\begin{lemma}[Sign criterion]\label{lem:sign}
Let $\rho > 0$, $C \ge 0$ be rational and $n \ge 1$.  Then
\[
L(\rho,C,n,\varepsilon) < 0 \iff n\rho^2 < C^2\ln(1/\varepsilon).
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Both directions reduce to comparing $C^2\cdot n\ln(1/\varepsilon)$ with
$(n\rho)^2 = n\cdot n\rho^2$ and dividing by $n > 0$, using
$C^2x \le y^2 \Rightarrow C\sqrt{x}\le y$ for $C,y \ge 0$ and its strict
converse.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Break-even block size]
$\displaystyle n^\ast(\rho,C,\varepsilon) = \frac{C^2\ln(1/\varepsilon)}{\rho^2}$.
\end{definition}

By Lemma~\ref{lem:sign} this is the exact sign change of $L$.

\begin{lemma}[Half-rate regime]\label{lem:half}
If $4C^2\ln(1/\varepsilon) \le n\rho^2$, i.e.\ $n \ge 4n^\ast$, then
$L(\rho,C,n,\varepsilon) \ge n\rho/2$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The hypothesis gives $C^2\cdot n\ln(1/\varepsilon) \le (n\rho/2)^2$, hence
$C\sqrt{n\ln(1/\varepsilon)} \le n\rho/2$; subtract from $n\rho$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Finite-key extraction]\label{thm:extract}
Let $\rho,C$ be rational, $n,k \in \mathbb{N}$, $\varepsilon > 0$.  Assume
\begin{itemize}
\item[($\AEP$)] $L(\rho,C,n,\varepsilon) \le k$, i.e.\ the adversary's
min-entropy about the reconciled raw key is at least $L$ bits;
\item $\Lext(\rho,C,n,\varepsilon) \ge 0$.
\end{itemize}
Then there is an output length $\ell \in \mathbb{N}$ with
\[
\ell \;\ge\; \Lext(\rho,C,n,\varepsilon) - 1
= n\rho - C\sqrt{n\ln(1/\varepsilon)} - 2\log_2(1/\varepsilon) - 1
\]
such that every distribution $p$ on the $\ell$-bit output with
$\sum_i p_i^2 \le 2^{-\ell} + 2^{-k}$ is $\varepsilon$-close to uniform in
statistical distance.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Take $\ell = \lfloor \Lext \rfloor$; then $\ell > \Lext - 1$.  For security,
$\ell \le \Lext = L - 2\log_2(1/\varepsilon) \le k - 2\log_2(1/\varepsilon)$ by
$\AEP$, which is the budget of Theorem~\ref{thm:lhl}.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The certified instance at $Q = 11\%$}

\begin{theorem}[Finite-key BB84 at $Q = 11\%$]\label{thm:fk11}
At a measured QBER of exactly $11\%$, with $C \ge 0$ and
$\varepsilon \in (0,1)$, any min-entropy budget $k$ compatible with $\AEP$ yields
an $\varepsilon$-secure extractable key of length at least
\[
\frac{n}{6000} - C\sqrt{n\ln(1/\varepsilon)} - 2\log_2(1/\varepsilon) - 1
\quad\text{bits.}
\]
Moreover the certified length never exceeds the true asymptotic budget
$n\,r(0.11)$.
\end{theorem}

Specialize $C = 10$, $\varepsilon = 2^{-50}$, so $\ln(1/\varepsilon) = 50\log 2
\approx 34.657$ and $\log_2(1/\varepsilon) = 50$.

\begin{theorem}[Empty guarantee below $10^{11}$]\label{thm:empty}
For every $n \le 10^{11}$, $L(1/6000, 10, n, 2^{-50}) \le 0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:sign} it suffices that $n(1/6000)^2 < 100\cdot 50\log 2$.  The
left side is at most $10^{11}/(3.6\times10^7) = 2777.8$; the right exceeds
$5000\cdot0.693 = 3465$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Recovery above $10^{12}$]\label{thm:recover}
For every $n \ge 10^{12}$, $L(1/6000,10,n,2^{-50}) \ge n/12000$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
It suffices that $10\sqrt{n\cdot50\log2} \le n/12000$, i.e.\
$100\cdot50\log2\cdot n \le n^2/(1.44\times10^8)$, which follows from
$\log 2 < 0.694$ and $n \ge 10^{12}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Parameter row at $Q=11\%$]\label{thm:row11}
For every $n \ge 10^{12}$ and every $k$ satisfying $\AEP$ there is an
$\varepsilon$-secure extractable key of length at least $n/12000 - 101$ bits, at
$\varepsilon = 2^{-50}$.  For instance $n = 10^{12}$ gives at least
$83\,333\,232$ bits and $n = 10^{14}$ at least $8\,333\,333\,232$ bits.
\end{theorem}

The exact break-even is $n^\ast = 100\cdot50\log2\cdot6000^2 = 1.2477\times
10^{11}$; the two theorems bracket it by factors of about $1.25$ and $8$, the
gap being the price of clean round constants.

\begin{remark}[Deployment moral]
At $Q = 11\%$ the asymptotic theory reports a positive rate and declares the link
secure.  The finite-key accounting reports nothing at all for any block below
$10^{11}$ sifted bits --- several years of continuous operation at megahertz
sifted rates.
\end{remark}

\section{The two-sided inverse-square law}
\label{sec:law}

\subsection{One-sided Lipschitz bounds for the binary entropy}

Both halves come from the same template: for a constant $K$, study monotonicity
of $p \mapsto H(p) - Kp$, whose derivative is $\log\frac{1-p}{p} - K$.

\begin{lemma}[Upper Lipschitz bound]\label{lem:lipup}
For $1/10 \le x \le y \le 1/2$, $\;H(y) - H(x) \le \log 9\,(y-x)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Let $g(p) = \log 9\cdot p - H(p)$ on $[1/10,1/2]$.  Then
$g'(p) = \log 9 - \log\frac{1-p}{p} \ge 0$ there, because $\frac{1-p}{p} \le 9$
for $p \ge 1/10$.  So $g$ is nondecreasing and $g(x) \le g(y)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Lower Lipschitz bound]\label{lem:lipdown}
For $0 < x \le y \le 1/2$,
$\;\log\frac{1-y}{y}\,(y-x) \le H(y) - H(x)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Set $K = \log\frac{1-y}{y}$ and $g(p) = H(p) - Kp$ on $[x,y]$.  Then
$g'(p) = \log\frac{1-p}{p} - K \ge 0$ on $[x,y]$, since $\log\frac{1-p}{p}$ is
decreasing and $p \le y$.  So $g$ is nondecreasing and $g(x) \le g(y)$.
\end{proof}

The lemmas differ only in whether $K$ sits above or below the range of $H'$ on
the interval.

\subsection{Linear vanishing at the threshold}

\begin{theorem}[At most linear vanishing]\label{thm:atmost}
Let $\Qstar \le 1/2$ be a threshold and $1/10 \le Q \le \Qstar$.  Then
$r_\nats(Q) \le 2\log 9\,(\Qstar - Q)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$r_\nats(Q) = r_\nats(Q) - r_\nats(\Qstar) = 2\big(H(\Qstar) - H(Q)\big)
\le 2\log9(\Qstar - Q)$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:lipup}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[At least linear vanishing]\label{thm:atleast}
Let $\Qstar \le 1/5$ be a threshold and $0 < Q \le \Qstar$.  Then
\[
r_\nats(Q) \;\ge\; 4\log 2\,(\Qstar - Q),
\qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad r(Q) \ge 4(\Qstar - Q)\ \text{bits.}
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$r_\nats(Q) = 2(H(\Qstar) - H(Q)) \ge 2\log\frac{1-\Qstar}{\Qstar}(\Qstar - Q)$
by Lemma~\ref{lem:lipdown}.  Since $\Qstar \le 1/5$ we have
$\frac{1-\Qstar}{\Qstar} \ge 4$, so
$\log\frac{1-\Qstar}{\Qstar} \ge \log 4 = 2\log 2$.
\end{proof}

The hypothesis $\Qstar \le 1/5$ is satisfied by the enclosure
$\Qstar < 0.1101$.  The constant is conservative: at $\Qstar = 0.110028$ the true
slope is $2\log\frac{1-\Qstar}{\Qstar} = 4.1809$ against the certified
$4\log 2 = 2.7726$.

\subsection{The law}

\begin{theorem}[Break-even from below]\label{thm:below}
Let $C > 0$ be rational, $\Qstar \le 1/2$ a threshold, $Q \in [1/10,\Qstar]$,
and let $\rho > 0$ be \emph{any} rational rate certificate valid at $Q$
(i.e.\ $\rho \le r(Q)$ in bits).  Then every $n$ with
$L(\rho,C,n,\varepsilon) > 0$ satisfies
\[
n \;\ge\; \frac{C^2\ln(1/\varepsilon)}{44\,(\Qstar - Q)^2}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Positivity of $L$ and Lemma~\ref{lem:sign} force
$C^2\ln(1/\varepsilon) \le n\rho^2$.  By Theorem~\ref{thm:atmost} and the
certified bounds $\log 9 \le 2.2726$, $\log 2 > 0.693$,
\[
\rho \;\le\; \frac{2\log 9}{\log 2}(\Qstar - Q) \;\le\; 6.6\,(\Qstar - Q),
\]
so $\rho^2 \le 44(\Qstar-Q)^2$; substitute.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Instance at $Q = 11\%$]\label{cor:below11}
With $C = 10$, $\varepsilon = 2^{-50}$, and the certified enclosure giving
$\Qstar - 0.11 < 10^{-4}$: no rational rate certificate at $Q = 11\%$ can produce
a positive finite-key length below $n = 7\times10^{9}$ sifted bits.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Break-even from above]\label{thm:above}
Let $C > 0$ be rational, $\ln(1/\varepsilon) \ge 0$, and let $\Qstar \le 1/5$ be
a threshold with $0 < Q < \Qstar$.  Then there is an explicit positive rational
$\rho$ with $\rho \le r(Q)$ in bits such that for every $n \ge 1$ with
$n \ge C^2\ln(1/\varepsilon)/\big(9(\Qstar - Q)^2\big)$ one has
$L(\rho,C,n,\varepsilon) > 0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Put $\delta = \Qstar - Q > 0$.  By Theorem~\ref{thm:atleast},
$r(Q) \ge 4\delta$ bits.  Choose any rational $\rho$ strictly between $3\delta$
and $4\delta$; it is valid and satisfies $\rho^2 > 9\delta^2$.  If
$n \ge C^2\ln(1/\varepsilon)/(9\delta^2)$ then
$C^2\ln(1/\varepsilon) \le 9n\delta^2 < n\rho^2$, and Lemma~\ref{lem:sign}
gives $L > 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Two-sided inverse-square law]\label{thm:twosided}
Let $C > 0$ be rational, $\ln(1/\varepsilon) \ge 0$, $\Qstar \le 1/5$ a
threshold, and $Q \in [1/10, \Qstar)$.  Then
\begin{itemize}
\item \emph{(lower)} every valid rational certificate $\rho$ at $Q$ has
break-even block size at least
$C^2\ln(1/\varepsilon)/\big(44(\Qstar-Q)^2\big)$;
\item \emph{(upper)} some valid rational certificate $\rho$ has break-even block
size at most $C^2\ln(1/\varepsilon)/\big(9(\Qstar-Q)^2\big)$.
\end{itemize}
Consequently $n^\ast(Q) = \Theta\big((\Qstar - Q)^{-2}\big)$, with certified
constants differing by the factor $44/9 < 5$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Theorems~\ref{thm:below} and~\ref{thm:above}.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The exponent $2$ is exact and forced by the $\sqrt{n}$ shape of the statistical
correction alone: the break-even condition $n\rho = C\sqrt{n\ln(1/\varepsilon)}$
gives $n \propto \rho^{-2}$ for any rate function, and the entropy enters only
through the linear vanishing $\rho \asymp \delta$.  This separation is why the
law is protocol-independent.  Both constants are conservative --- $44$
over-estimates $(2\log9/\log2)^2 = 40.2$, and $9$ under-estimates the achievable
$\approx36$ near $\Qstar$.  With the sharp constants $40.19$ and $36.38$ the
ratio would be only $1.10$, so essentially all of the certified factor $4.9$ is
recoverable slack rather than genuine uncertainty about $n^\ast$.
\end{remark}

\begin{corollary}[Explicit two-sided instance at $Q = 10\%$]
With $C = 10$, $\varepsilon = 2^{-50}$ and the enclosure $\Qstar > 0.1100$
giving $\delta > 1/100$, the upper half produces an explicit positive rational
certificate whose break-even block size is below $4\times10^{6}$ sifted bits.  No
irrational or floating-point data enter the construction.
\end{corollary}

