\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Algorithm 1 (rate certificate search).}
Input: integers $a,c>0$, a denominator budget $\den$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item Form the exact integers $N = 2^{a+c}a^{2a}c^{2c}$, $D = (a+c)^{2(a+c)}$.
\item Compute the largest $m$ with $2^m D \le N$ by doubling; this is
$m = \lfloor (a+c) r\rfloor$.
\item Set $y = N/(2^mD)$ exactly and take $\num = \lfloor \den(y-1)\rfloor$.
\item Verify the single integer inequality
$(\den + \num)2^mD \le \den N$.
\item Return $\big(m\log2 + 2\num/(2\den+\num)\big)/(a+c)$ nats.
\end{enumerate}
Cost is dominated by forming $N$ and $D$, i.e.\ $O\big((a+c)\log(a+c)\big)$-bit
integers and a handful of big-integer multiplications; all comparisons are exact.

\paragraph{Algorithm 2 (break-even and table row).}
Input: rational $\rho \in (0,1]$, rational $C$, security parameter $\varepsilon$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item $n^\ast \leftarrow C^2\ln(1/\varepsilon)/\rho^2$.
\item For $n < n^\ast$ report ``no guarantee'' (Lemma~\ref{lem:sign} shows the
length is genuinely negative, not merely unproven).
\item For $n \ge 4n^\ast$ report $n\rho/2 - 2\log_2(1/\varepsilon) - 1$.
\item In the band $n^\ast \le n < 4n^\ast$ report the exact
$n\rho - C\sqrt{n\ln(1/\varepsilon)} - 2\log_2(1/\varepsilon) - 1$.
\end{enumerate}

\paragraph{Algorithm 3 (two-sided bracket).}
Input: $Q \in [1/10,\Qstar)$, parameters $C,\varepsilon$, a rational enclosure
$[\underline{Q},\overline{Q}] \ni \Qstar$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\delta_{\min} \leftarrow \underline{Q} - Q$,
$\delta_{\max} \leftarrow \overline{Q} - Q$.
\item Lower bound $C^2\ln(1/\varepsilon)/(44\delta_{\max}^2)$.
\item Upper bound $C^2\ln(1/\varepsilon)/(9\delta_{\min}^2)$.
\item Optionally exhibit the witness: any rational in
$(3\delta_{\min}, 4\delta_{\min}]$.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Discussion}

\paragraph{Why exact arithmetic.}
Near a threshold the quantity of interest is a difference of large nearly-equal
quantities, exactly where floating-point evaluation is least trustworthy.  At
$Q = 11\%$ the rate $1.68\times10^{-4}$ is obtained as $1 - 2\times0.49991596$:
four significant digits of cancellation.  Replacing this with integer
comparisons costs a little sharpness --- the certified $1/6000$ against the true
$1/5949$, a loss of $0.8\%$ --- and buys unconditional confidence.  The loss is
systematically reducible: the hybrid residual is $O((y-1)^3)$, so a single
dyadic step already brings the certificate within a few parts in $10^5$.

\paragraph{The right figure of merit.}
Replace the acceptance criterion $Q < \Qstar$ by a break-even computation: from a
defensible certificate $\rho$ and the operationally required $\varepsilon$ and
$C$, compute $n^\ast = C^2\ln(1/\varepsilon)/\rho^2$ and compare it with the
block size the hardware delivers in the operational window.
Theorem~\ref{thm:twosided} gives the order of magnitude without any computation:
within a factor of five, $n^\ast$ is $C^2\ln(1/\varepsilon)$ divided by roughly
$20(\Qstar-Q)^2$.

\paragraph{Limitations.}
The correction $C\sqrt{n\ln(1/\varepsilon)}$ is a model of the finite-size
statistical penalty, not a derivation from a specific entropy-estimation
argument; what is proved is the accounting.  Different estimation techniques
change $C$, none change the exponent in Theorem~\ref{thm:twosided}.  The lower
half of the law is stated for $Q \ge 1/10$ because $\log\frac{1-p}{p}$ diverges
as $p \to 0$ --- precisely the regime where the rate is large and finite-key
corrections are irrelevant.  The upper half needs only $Q > 0$ and
$\Qstar \le 1/5$.  The threshold enters only as ``$\Qstar$ is a zero of the rate
in the stated range''.  The hypothesis $\rho \le 1$ in
Theorem~\ref{thm:tablerow} is harmless, since no BB84 rate exceeds one bit per
sifted bit.

\section{Future directions}

\paragraph{1. Geometric convergence of iterated root certificates.}
The hybrid bound $\log(N/D) \ge m\log2 + 2(y-1)/(y+1)$ has residual error
$O((y-1)^3)$, and $\log y = 2^k\log\big(y^{1/2^k}\big)$.  Applying the Pad\'e
step at the $k$-th square root should shrink the certificate error like $4^{-k}$
while each step remains a single integer comparison on $N^{2^k}$ against a
dyadic multiple of $D^{2^k}$.  The conjecture is that $k$ steps certify the rate
to relative accuracy $4^{-k}$ at a certificate bit-cost linear in $2^k$.  The
$k=0$ case is Theorem~\ref{thm:hybrid}, and the observed residual at $Q = 10\%$
($4.5\times10^{-6}$ bits per sifted bit against the dyadic $2.0\times10^{-3}$)
already matches the predicted cubic law to one digit.

\paragraph{2. Optimal rational approximation of the threshold.}
The enclosure $\Qstar \in (0.1100, 0.1101)$ is produced by integer comparisons
whose bit-length grows like $(a+c)\log(a+c)$.  The number of correct decimal
digits obtained per unit of certificate size should obey an exact yield law,
making certificate cost predictable rather than empirical.

\paragraph{3. Sharpening the two-sided constants.}
The certified ratio $44/9$ has two identifiable sources of slack: the
conservative slope $4\log2 = 2.7726$ in place of the true
$2\log\frac{1-\Qstar}{\Qstar} = 4.1809$, and the $3\delta$ versus $4\delta$
rational-approximation step.  Removing both should bring the ratio below
$44/36 \approx 1.2$, at which point the two bounds essentially determine
$n^\ast$.

\paragraph{4. Protocol variants.}
The separation argument --- exponent from the $\sqrt{n}$ correction, constant
from the entropy --- suggests the same two-sided law holds verbatim for
decoy-state BB84, six-state, and entanglement-based protocols, with $\Qstar$ and
the Lipschitz constants replaced by the corresponding protocol data.

\paragraph{5. Second-order finite-key models.}
Refined analyses replace $C\sqrt{n\ln(1/\varepsilon)}$ by an expansion with a
$\log n$ term.  Determining how such a correction perturbs the two-sided law ---
the expectation being that the exponent survives and only the constants move ---
would complete the picture.

\section{Conclusion}

The eleven-percent threshold of BB84 is a correct asymptotic statement and a poor
engineering criterion.  Quantitatively: at a measured QBER of exactly $11\%$,
with standard parameters, the certified extractable key length is non-positive
for every block size up to $10^{11}$ sifted bits, and recovers half the
asymptotic budget only past $10^{12}$.  Structurally: the break-even block size
obeys a two-sided inverse-square law $n^\ast(Q) = \Theta\big((\Qstar-Q)^{-2}\big)$
with certified constants $44$ and $9$, so the divergence is a genuine feature of
finite-key accounting and not an artifact of any particular estimate.

Underlying both is a certificate calculus in which every rate bound is an exact
integer comparison --- dyadic, Pad\'e, or hybrid --- so that the chain from a
$400$-digit integer to a parameter table contains no floating-point arithmetic.
Along the way, a commonly stated form of the privacy-amplification bound was
found to have unsatisfiable hypotheses in its advertised regime, and was replaced
by a version with the same conclusion, satisfiable hypotheses, and an exact
algebraic derivation of the leftover-hash budget.

The recommendation to practitioners is simple: report break-even block sizes, not
threshold margins.

